<clickhouse>
    <!--
      An explicit server memory ceiling, set below the container's own limit.

      HISTORY. This file first set max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio to 4, to work around a
      server-level memory tracker that over-counted badly: it reported 5.40 GiB committed while
      the process RSS sat at 1.15 GiB, and queries were killed non-deterministically by the
      OvercommitTracker while the container had gigabytes free.

      That fixed the symptom and created a worse problem. A ratio of 4 tells the server it may
      use four times the VM's RAM, so it does not self-limit at all - and background merges after
      the daily-partitioning migration grew until the container reached its 6 GiB cgroup limit
      and the whole server stopped responding. Nothing was killed; it simply thrashed, and even
      `SELECT 1` timed out for minutes.

      A server that consumes everything and then stops answering is misconfigured, not unlucky.

      THE FIX. An absolute ceiling, below the container limit, so ClickHouse throttles itself
      rather than being throttled by the kernel. 4.5 GiB against a 6 GiB container leaves the
      remainder for page cache and the OS.

      Per-query ceilings still matter and are still set by the jobs (max_memory_usage with
      spill-to-disk below it). This bounds the server as a whole, including merges, which no
      per-query setting can reach.

      WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE. An earlier revision also lowered background_pool_size to
      reduce merge concurrency. ClickHouse refused to start: merge_tree's
      number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation defaults to 20 and must not exceed the
      pool, so shrinking the pool means retuning several coupled settings. The ceiling above
      already makes the server throttle its own merges as it approaches the limit, which is the
      behaviour that was wanted - so the pool is left alone rather than tuned into a knot.

      IN PRODUCTION this should be roughly 70-80% of the container's limit, expressed the same
      way: an explicit number, matched to the memory the container is actually given.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>4831838208</max_server_memory_usage>
</clickhouse>
